Project: Django. Upgrade: 3.2 -> 4.2. Release of these items: 4.0.
Source: Django 4.0 release notes.

Label every item:
- risk = the change could break an application or deployment, require migration work, or create operational risk for SOME user upgrading across this release (removed or renamed APIs/settings/flags, changed defaults, behavior changes, raised minimum versions of runtimes/databases/dependencies, data or schema migration, required config changes, deprecated-and-removed features).
- context = accurate and useful but needs no user action at this release: explicitly "no user action required", a deprecation that still works (warning only), a purely additive new feature or option, documentation pointers.
- noise = credits, install/download commands, pure bug fixes that cannot change correct behavior, marketing text, headings with no content.

Return one label per item_id with a one-sentence reason grounded in the item text.

Items (JSON lines):
{"item_id": "01ced70c389c", "section": "What's new in Django 4.0 / Minor features / Tests", "text": "The test --parallel option now supports the value auto to run one test process for each processor core."}
{"item_id": "6e15254fdcf0", "section": "What's new in Django 4.0 / Minor features / Tests", "text": "TestCase.captureOnCommitCallbacks now captures new callbacks added while executing transaction.on_commit callbacks."}
{"item_id": "d96625116e96", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / Database backend API", "text": "This section describes changes that may be needed in third-party database backends."}
{"item_id": "d82af4e3c847", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / Database backend API", "text": "DatabaseOperations.year_lookup_bounds_for_date_field() and year_lookup_bounds_for_datetime_field() methods now take the optional iso_year argument in order to support bounds for ISO-8601 week-numbering years."}
{"item_id": "a344975cc491", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / Database backend API", "text": "The second argument of DatabaseSchemaEditor._unique_sql() and _create_unique_sql() methods is now fields instead of columns."}
{"item_id": "3b393a0bcf8d", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / django.contrib.gis", "text": "Support for PostGIS 2.3 is removed."}
{"item_id": "9b95ae21c41f", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / django.contrib.gis", "text": "Support for GDAL 2.0 and GEOS 3.5 is removed."}
{"item_id": "954d4be5adb3", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / Dropped support for PostgreSQL 9.6", "text": "Upstream support for PostgreSQL 9.6 ends in November 2021. Django 4.0 supports PostgreSQL 10 and higher. Also, the minimum supported version of psycopg2 is increased from 2.5.4 to 2.8.4, as psycopg2 2.8.4 is the first release to support Python 3.8."}
{"item_id": "180b6dc87be4", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / Dropped support for Oracle 12.2 and 18c", "text": "Upstream support for Oracle 12.2 ends in March 2022 and for Oracle 18c it ends in June 2021. Django 3.2 will be supported until April 2024. Django 4.0 officially supports Oracle 19c."}
{"item_id": "4e9aa14e976b", "section": "Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS changes / Format change", "text": "Values in the CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS setting must include the scheme (e.g. 'http://' or 'https://') instead of only the hostname. Also, values that started with a dot, must now also include an asterisk before the dot. For example, change '.example.com' to 'https://*.example.com'. A system check detects any required changes."}
{"item_id": "2808b6b0cd8f", "section": "Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS changes / Configuring it may now be required", "text": "As CSRF protection now consults the Origin header, you may need to set CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS, particularly if you allow requests from subdomains by setting CSRF_COOKIE_DOMAIN (or SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN if CSRF_USE_SESSIONS is enabled) to a value starting with a dot."}
{"item_id": "fd2373690a83", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / SecurityMiddleware no longer sets the X-XSS-Protection header", "text": "The SecurityMiddleware no longer sets the X-XSS-Protection header if the SECURE_BROWSER_XSS_FILTER setting is True. The setting is removed. Most modern browsers don't honor the X-XSS-Protection HTTP header. You can use Content-Security-Policy_ without allowing 'unsafe-inline' scripts instead. If you want to support legacy browsers and set the header, use this line in a custom middleware: response.headers.setdefault(\"X-XSS-Protection\", \"1; mode=block\")"}
{"item_id": "619ff7f40b0a", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / Migrations autodetector changes", "text": "The migrations autodetector now uses model states instead of model classes. Also, migration operations for ForeignKey and ManyToManyField fields no longer specify attributes which were not passed to the fields during initialization. As a side-effect, running makemigrations might generate no-op AlterField operations for ManyToManyField and ForeignKey fields in some cases."}
{"item_id": "be1adc54b6e0", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / DeleteView changes", "text": "DeleteView now uses FormMixin to handle POST requests. As a consequence, any custom deletion logic in delete() handlers should be moved to form_valid(), or a shared helper method, if required."}
{"item_id": "a560c1d93108", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / Table and column naming scheme changes on Oracle", "text": "Django 4.0 inadvertently changed the table and column naming scheme on Oracle. This causes errors for models and fields with names longer than 30 characters. Unfortunately, renaming some Oracle tables and columns is required. Use the upgrade script in #33789 to generate RENAME statements to change naming scheme."}
{"item_id": "a91f7d73b3fe", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / Miscellaneous", "text": "Support for cx_Oracle < 7.0 is removed."}
{"item_id": "8bdce39513d6", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / Miscellaneous", "text": "To allow serving a Django site on a subpath without changing the value of STATIC_URL, the leading slash is removed from that setting (now 'static/') in the default startproject template."}
{"item_id": "48124bb77b73", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / Miscellaneous", "text": "The AdminSite method for the admin index view is no longer decorated with never_cache when accessed directly, rather than via the recommended AdminSite.urls property, or AdminSite.get_urls() method."}
{"item_id": "5734d3967f73", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / Miscellaneous", "text": "Unsupported operations on a sliced queryset now raise TypeError instead of AssertionError."}
{"item_id": "bfc5a83b99f4", "section": "Django 4.0 release notes / Backwards incompatible changes in 4.0 / Miscellaneous", "text": "The undocumented django.test.runner.reorder_suite() function is renamed to reorder_tests(). It now accepts an iterable of tests rather than a test suite, and returns an iterator of tests."}
